# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses b350a3107fa4, 2026-10-19
opp	two	opposites	P2	comparative or contrary opposites with degrees between them (greater/less, weaker/stronger), where a thing passes from one to the other by increase or decrease	contradictory states with nothing between (alive vs not alive), where nothing becomes "more dead" by degrees
genopp	one
living_from_dead	one
souls_below	one
# The examples behind P1 (S4-S16) are all comparatives or contraries that admit intermediate processes like waxing and waning. In P2 (S31), life and death are opposites only as contradictories.
# Life and death can be opposites in the second sense and still fall outside the first, which is the class P1's generalisation was drawn from.
# No single P or A joins the two senses. The shift happens when P1, read as covering only the first kind of opposite, is applied through P2 to a pair of the second kind. So I name no bridge.
# I was unsure whether "generated" also shifts, from a comparative change of degree to a living thing coming into being out of dead ones. I left genopp as one sense because the text keeps the same wording from S17 through S33.
